{
  "updated_at": "2026-10-07 11:09 JST",
  "total": 50,
  "toyota_summary": "Europe's center-right bloc stopped pushing to kill the EU's 2035 combustion engine ban. The European People's Party, which controls significant EU legislative power, had previously campaigned to overturn the rule but reversed course and is now only seeking more flexibility for automakers to meet emissions targets. This removes a major political threat to one of the industry's most consequential regulations, though negotiations over implementation details continue.\n\nThe shift matters because it signals the ban will likely hold. Automakers had spent years lobbying to either kill the rule outright or delay it, betting on political pressure from conservative parties. That strategy has failed. The industry now faces a hard deadline for ending combustion engine sales across Europe and must accelerate electrification investments accordingly.\n\nWatch whether automakers pivot their lobbying to securing easier credit rules or extension periods within the existing framework rather than challenging the ban itself.",
